<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Province of Quebec</h1><p class="st-meta">1763 CE – 1791 CE · capital Quebec</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Britain's first government of conquered French Canada, whose Quebec Act of 1774 protected Catholic religion and French civil law.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the British conquest of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-france">New France</a> in 1759 to 1760, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1763">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1763 ceded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-canada">Canada</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-proclamation-of-1763">Royal Proclamation</a> of that year organised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-lawrence-river">St Lawrence</a> settlements as the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>. Its first civil governor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-murray-governor-of-quebec">James Murray</a>, soon found that plans to anglicise the French-speaking Catholic population were impractical.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec-act">Quebec Act</a> of 1774 extended the province into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a> country, allowed Catholics to hold office, kept the seigneurial system and French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a>, and recognised the Church's right to collect tithes. Government lay with a governor and appointed council, with no elected assembly. The economy rested on farming and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a> out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>. American forces invaded in 1775 but failed to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec City</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The arrival of thousands of Loyalists after 1783 created pressure for English law and representative government. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-act-1791">Constitutional Act</a> of 1791 divided the province into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lower-canada">Lower Canada</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/upper-canada">Upper Canada</a>, each with an elected assembly.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/province-of-quebec.jpg" alt="Province of Quebec" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: René Chaloult Vector: Krun · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Quebec.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1763 CE – 1791 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Quebec</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>James Murray, Guy Carleton, Frederick Haldimand</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Quebec (1763-1791)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1777&amp;focus=province-of-quebec" aria-label="Province of Quebec on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3057 381 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Province of Quebec lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3377 637L3327 789M3377 637L4997 651M3377 637L4997 651M3377 637L3092 886M3377 637L3419 694"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3327 789h0M4997 651h0M4997 651h0M3092 886h0M3419 694h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3377 637h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3377 637h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1777&amp;focus=province-of-quebec">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
